chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter states that the reward halves every 210,000 blocks, from 50 coins in 2009 to 25 in 2012, 12.5 in 2016 and 6.25 in 2020 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The saved blocks confirm each date and reward: block 210,000 on 2012-11-28, block 420,000 on 2016-07-09, block 630,000 on 2020-05-11 and block 840,000 on 2024-04-20 at 00:09:27 UTC, the first to pay 3.125 coins, and the subsidy function of the node shifts the integer subsidy right once per interval (Mempool Space, 2026); (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P74, chx:P75, chx:P76, chx:P77, chx:P28 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The supply cannot exceed 21 million coins: the sum of the 33 non-zero rewards is 2,099,999,997,690,000 satoshis, 2,310,000 satoshis short of 21 million coins, and the last block with a reward of 1 satoshi is 6,929,999, which supports the book's 'approximately the year 2140' (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P59 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Erratum of the book's code: max_money.py divides the reward with current_reward /= 2, a float division in Python 3, and run as printed it goes on for 1,107 steps and prints 2100000000000000.0 and not the 2099999997690000 of the text; with the integer division the book's figure appears (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P09, chx:P01 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Erratum of spelling: the book's toy proof of work says that 'Hello, World! 32' produces the hash 09cb91f8..., but the hash with a capital W is 6bbe6a3d...; the shell example in the same section uses a lowercase w, gives the printed hash and finds its first zero at the counter 32 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Erratum of arithmetic: the book says that at the difficulty of block 277,316 a miner doing 1 TH/s would find a block once in 8,496 blocks or 59 days; the difficulty 1,180,923,195 times 2 to the 32 divided by 10 to the 12 gives 58.7 days or 8,453 blocks, about half a percent fewer, and the hash of the block, with 63 leading zero bits, agrees with the book's 'more than 60' (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Mempool Space, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P72 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Changed state: the book says that fees are usually a small percentage of income, but block 840,000, mined by ViaBTC, paid 3,762,561,499 satoshis in fees against a subsidy of 312,500,000, 12.04 times as much, in 3,050 transactions; the claim is a description of the period of writing and not a law, and the book itself says that fees will matter more as the reward falls (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Mempool Space, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P74 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Changed default: the coinbase transaction made by Bitcoin Core 31.1 has nLockTime equal to the height minus 1, nSequence 0xfffffffe and a script that ends with OP_0, none of which is in the book's table; the locktime rule is the one of BIP 54 and keeps new coinbases different from the early violations of BIP 34 (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Poinsot and Corallo, 2025); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P34, chx:P20, chx:P01 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Changed default: since release 29.0 the block reserved weight is a single option, -blockreservedweight, default 8,000 weight units (the fixed 4,000 had been reserved twice by a bug), so a Bitcoin Core template never exceeds 3,992,000; block 840,000 weighed 3,993,281, so it was made by other software, and release 29.0 also stopped applying the BIP 94 timewarp mitigation on regtest (Bitcoin Core developers, 2025); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Mempool Space, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P44, chx:P39, chx:P74 .
chx:F9 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The retarget rule of pow.cpp reproduces the real adjustment of 2024: from the headers of blocks 838,656 and 840,671, 1,186,047 seconds apart, and the bits 17034219, it computes 170331db, the bits of block 840,672; the 2,015-gap measurement makes the aim 0.05 percent faster than 10 minutes, and the clamp to a quarter and four times the two weeks works as recomputed (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Mempool Space, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P26, chx:P80, chx:P81, chx:P82 .
chx:F10 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Changed code: the book's listing of the retarget function has no branch for testnet4; Bitcoin Core 31.1 has if (params.enforce_BIP94), true for testnet4 and false for mainnet, which builds the new target from the bits of the first block of the period, because that block may not use the minimum-difficulty exception (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Jahr, 2024)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P26, chx:P21 .
chx:F11 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The version field is no longer a version: block 277,316 has 2, block 709,632 has 0x20200004 (bit 2 signals taproot) and block 840,000 has 0x2a5fe000, with rolled bits 0x0a5fe000 inside the mask 0x1fffe000 of BIP 320, a draft for which BIP 323 is proposed as replacement; 65,536 values times 2 to the 32 nonces give the 281 TH/s of the book, and a block with a rolled version was accepted on the test chain (BtcDrak, 2018); (Mempool Space, 2026); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P19, chx:P74, chx:P01 .
chx:F12 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The consensus checks are not only described but run: thirteen transaction cases and fourteen block cases on a Bitcoin Core 31.1 node each gave a named reason, apart from one transaction with no inputs that could not even be decoded (bad-txns-premature-spend-of-coinbase at depth 1 and 99 and success at 100, bad-cb-amount, bad-cb-height, bad-cb-length for 1 and 101 bytes, time-too-old, time-too-new, bad-txnmrklroot, high-hash, bad-diffbits, bad-blk-length), and the reasons are defined in the validation source (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P41, chx:P29, chx:P01 .
chx:F13 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chain selection is by cumulative work and not by length: on two test nodes a payment of 49.999 coins with one confirmation went to -2 confirmations when a competing branch with chainwork 208 against 206 replaced it, Carol had received 0 and the refund had 2 confirmations; the book's definition of the best chain as the one with the most cumulative proof of work agrees, and Rosenfeld's analysis gives the odds of such a reversal (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Rosenfeld, 2014)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P51 .
chx:F14 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Median time past and the two-hour rule are both enforced: a test block equal to the median was refused as time-too-old and one two hours and a second ahead as time-too-new, MAX_FUTURE_BLOCK_TIME is 2 * 60 * 60 in chain.h, and BIP 113 uses the median of the last 11 blocks; Optech notes that a time warp attack makes the median time past increase much more slowly than normal (Kerin and Friedenbach, 2015); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P15, chx:P40, chx:P52 .
chx:F15 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Mining pools have a concentration that the book could not know: in the last year, of 52,371 blocks, Foundry USA found 28.2 percent, AntPool 18.2, F2Pool 12.6 and ViaBTC 9.7, so four pools found 68.6 percent; the book's remark that a miner with 1 percent of the hash rate finds more than a block a day (1.44) is right, while one with 0.01 percent waits 69 days on average (Mempool Space, 2026); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P85, chx:P01 .
chx:F16 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Hash rate has risen beyond the book's examples: the difficulty of 132,716,002,350,731 implies 9.5e20 hashes per second, mempool.space reports 1.029e21 and blockchain.com 1.003e21, which is 121,738 times the 8.45e15 implied by the difficulty of block 277,316, the block of the book's example (Mempool Space, 2026); (Blockchain Charts, 2026); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P86, chx:P88, chx:P83, chx:P01 .
chx:F17 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The threshold of attack is lower than the book's headline: the book says that a '51% attack' does not need 51 percent, and the paper on selfish mining proves that the upper bound of the threshold is one third, with one quarter for a pool whose blocks are followed by half of the honest miners; Nakamoto's own table gives 0.0002428 for a 10 percent attacker 6 blocks behind, which was recomputed, and Optech summarises selfish mining (Eyal and Sirer, 2013); (Nakamoto, 2008); (Bitcoin Optech, 2026); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P50, chx:P47, chx:P54, chx:P01 .
chx:F18 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The book does not discuss block withholding, an attack against pools in which a member is paid for shares but never submits a share that would be a full block and keeps 99.9 percent of his expected revenue; Optech names oblivious shares as a proposed remedy, and Stratum V2, which lets miners declare their own templates, changes who chooses the transactions (Bitcoin Optech, 2026); (Stratum contributors, 2026); (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P56, chx:P66, chx:P61 .
chx:F19 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Changed ecosystem: Bitcoin Core 30.0 added an experimental IPC mining interface that lets a node work with Stratum v2 or other client software, started with -ipcbind and the bitcoin command; the 31.1 help lists no -ipcbind option for bitcoind itself, so the interface was not executed here (Bitcoin Core developers, 2025); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P45, chx:P46 .
chx:F20 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chain split of March 2013 is mentioned by the book in one sentence; the saved data give its numbers: block 225,430, 797 transactions and 249,119 bytes, at 2013-03-11 23:24:19 UTC, and a split that lasted 24 blocks to block 225,454 at 06:19:32 UTC the next morning, caused by the Berkeley DB lock limit of versions before 0.8; the pools of Slush and BTCGuild went back to 0.7 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Andresen, 2013); (Buterin, 2013); (Mempool Space, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P22, chx:P89, chx:P78, chx:P91 .
chx:F21 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The 80 to 20 hard-fork arithmetic of the book is right: 12.5 minutes per block and 25,200 minutes, 17.5 days, for 2,016 blocks on the 80 percent chain, and 50 minutes per block and 70 days on the 20 percent chain; the Bitcoin Cash split of 1 August 2017 at block 478,559 is an intended example (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Wikipedia contributors, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P71 .
chx:F22 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Soft forks were recomputed on test nodes: a lax node accepted a block with no height in the coinbase that a strict node refused with bad-cb-height, the strict node could not follow the lax chain, and the lax node reorganized onto the longer strict chain; BIP 34 itself counted 750 and 950 of 1,000 blocks, the numbers of the book, and is now a height in the code, 227,931 on mainnet (Andresen, 2012); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P12, chx:P33, chx:P01 .
chx:F23 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "BIP 9 is confirmed in a state machine run on the test chain: with a period of 144 and a threshold of 108 the deployment was defined at 143, started at 144, locked_in at 288 and active at 432, and a deployment with a timeout went to failed; the mainnet threshold is 1,916 of 2,016, on bits 0 to 28, and the node's source names the same states (Wuille et al., 2015); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P10, chx:P37, chx:P01 .
chx:F24 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Activation methods after BIP 9 are in the chain data: segwit was activated on 24 August 2017 at block 481,824 after BIP 91 and BIP 148 were proposed; BIP 8 adds a lock-in by height; Speedy Trial for taproot opened on 2021-04-24, timed out on 2021-08-11 with a threshold of 1,815 of 2,016 and a minimum activation height of 709,632, and block 709,632 was mined by F2Pool on 2021-11-14 (Hilliard, 2017); (Shaolinfry, 2017); (Fry and Dashjr, 2017); (Wuille et al., 2020); (Mempool Space, 2026); (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P18, chx:P17, chx:P11, chx:P23, chx:P79, chx:P53 .
chx:F25 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The book was written before the consensus cleanup of BIP 54, which would fix the time warp attack, make a transaction invalid if it has more than 2,500 signature operations over its inputs, forbid transactions of exactly 64 bytes, and set the coinbase locktime; it is a proposal, and the release notes of Bitcoin Core 30.0 say that a change was made to prepare for a possible deployment (Poinsot and Corallo, 2025); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2025); (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P20, chx:P45, chx:P52 .
chx:F26 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The idea of proof of work is older than Bitcoin and the book's toy example comes from it: Dwork and Naor proposed pricing functions against junk mail in 1992, Back proposed Hashcash in 1997 and wrote it up in 2002, and Nakamoto's paper cites Hashcash; the Bitcoin Wiki says that all Bitcoin miners create hashcash proofs of work (Dwork and Naor, 1992); (Back, 2002); (Nakamoto, 2008); (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026); (Wikipedia contributors, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P49, chx:P48, chx:P47, chx:P62, chx:P67, chx:P68 .
chx:F27 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Taken together, the chapter's account of mining, verification and the changing of rules stands, and nearly every claim could be recomputed. What is wrong or out of date lies at the edges: one float division in the supplied script, one capital letter, one ratio of blocks to days, the comfortable remark on fees, the old retarget listing and the field of the coinbase, and what has changed since the book is the concentration of the pools, the hash rate, the block reserved weight, the IPC mining interface and the proposed consensus cleanup (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P46 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "43 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "91 sources, all opened and verified at 2026-10-15T11:58:34Z; 57 cited by findings." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
